[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about the covering system approach. We want to find a covering [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msystem of congruences for n, and for each congruence class assign a prime p such[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthat p | k·2^n + 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf n ≡ a_i (mod m_i) is a covering system, and we want p_i | k·2^n + 1 whenever [0m[38;2;124;124;124mn ≡ a_i (mod m_i).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis means k·2^a_i ≡ -1 (mod p_i), i.e., k ≡ -2^{-a_i} (mod p_i).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe need: ord_{p_i}(2) | m_i (so that the condition is consistent on the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcongruence class), and actually we need 2^{m_i} ≡ 1 (mod p_i), and k·2^{a_i} ≡ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m-1 (mod p_i).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBy CRT, we can find k satisfying k ≡ -2^{-a_i} (mod p_i) for all i, provided the[0m[38;2;124;124;124mp_i are distinct primes.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe classic example: Sierpinski number. The smallest known is k = 78557, but the[0m[38;2;124;124;124mexistence proof uses a specific covering.[0m

